1 Introduction

A non-touch system is an advanced computer interface technology used to enhance humancomputer interaction (HCI) [1]. The interface helps to interact with a computer, machine or a robot in case of an unavoidable situation, security and treatment or industrial life. For the advancement of sensors and cameras, the non-touch devices and interfaces will continue to integrate into daily life. In recent years, HCI has improved the performance of applications involving gesture recognition. In particular, hand gesture recognition plays an important role as a technique for the development of HCI, since it provides a natural and direct way for users to express their feelings [2–5], interact with virtual reality (VR) [6] or augmented reality (AR) [7] devices, humanmachine interaction [8], and to play games [9, 10]. Many prior studies have also focused on the recognition of device-based gestures. This type of processing requires specialized hardware and dedicated devices [11, 12], or wearable devices [13], and these technologies are inconvenient and difficult to use in real life since they require users to be in direct contact with additional devices. However, both touch and non-touch devices have become widespread in various types of accessible devices, and especially in tablets, PCs and smartphones. A keyboard and mouse are usually used to input characters to computers, and software keyboards to input characters to smartphones or tablets. However, users may feel unsafe using these touch-based devices for sensitive applications, as hackers can collect user data and even biometrics, which may cause security problems. In non-touch techniques, however, the user does not need to type on a keyboard, write on a trackpad/touch screen, or control a smart system in many applications involving text applications. There is also a huge demand in the industry to provide advanced and comprehensive services to ensure a healthy and safe environment, including avoiding risky situations. In order to meet the highest demand, many industry applications have used advanced human-machine interface (HMI) technology [14]. Inspired by this technology, gesture recognition has been customized for a non-touch character input system and this has improved the progress of such applications. Shin and Kim proposed a non-touch character input method based on handtapping gestures to input English and Japanese characters, which can be used to simplify HCI [15]. Hand-tapping gestures can be effectively used as an alphabet, and this is accomplished using an aerial virtual keypad. However, it is difficult for the user to remember the input characters for the hand tapping gestures, and it is also difficult to detect the fingertip when the input of the hand tapping gestures overlaps the fingers. Consequently, in this paper, we propose a non-touch character input system that incorporates hand gesture recognition, where seven different gesture functions (i.e. newline, space, delete, backspace, open, close, and select language) can be recognized with a high level of accuracy in a real working environment. To achieve these goals, we need to solve two major underlying challenges:

1) Hand gesture recognition gives rise to numerous errors in an uncontrolled environment, since it relies on real-time processing, and thus requires an effective and practical technique for implementing these interfaces.
2) Hand-gesture-based non-touch interfaces and writing techniques are required in order to provide a more intuitive HCI with a virtual keyboard and gestural flick input system.

The points of focus and research contributions of this study are as follows:

1) A non-touch interface based hand gesture recognition system is introduced in an uncontrolled environment. We apply threshold masking to deal with the use of uncontrolled

