2 Related work
Today, non-touch devices and interfaces are becoming more diversified and are undergoing continued development. Traditionally, a keyboard and mouse have been used as input devices for computers; however, researchers are developing other forms of interaction via HCI where speed and hand tracking are important for interactive applications. Gesture recognition [16] provides a simple and intuitive approach to these interactions. However, the recognition of different shapes and directions of gestures even for the same person, and the use of image processing techniques that are complex and require overhead processing. Many researchers aim to use simple gestures for computer control and to provide a system for inputting characters [17] or recognizing letters/words [18, 19]. It consists of gesture modeling, analysis and recognition using machine and deep learning techniques, and is often applied to groups with specific needs. However, overlapping finger attempts may concern the effectiveness of gesture recognition. Besides, the author proposed glove based tracking to track the hand [20]. However, it is uncomfortable for long time use. In [21], authors proposed an eye-writing inputs system for HCI. These methods provide an alternative direction for the input system, and more accurate research is needed for recognition. A system of continuous recognition of text written in the air based on accelerometers and gyroscope sensors that serve as an input device for wearable computers [22]. However, there is a noise associated with data that makes it difficult to process incoming data classifications, also it is inconvenient to connect a device at all times.
Li et al. [23] presented a system that enables lecturers to write and control fluently on a nontouch screen via visible in-air gestures. However, these systems are limited by the complexity of the tablet system. There are many methods that have been used to determine the location of the palm, such as finger detection, joint location or identification of anchor points [24, 25]. In [26], the author proposed a distance metric to handle noise from the Kinect sensor, which measures the inequality in hand shapes. However, the shape recognition method is not robust to significant distortion of the hand shapes. Murata and Shin proposed a DP-matching-based numeric and alphabet character recognition system [27]. The image processing in this problem

