3 Proposed model
This section presents the proposed model and the basic workflow of our system. The input information is acquired via a webcam and processed using the proposed model. The workflow is divided into three segments: a) a virtual keyboard; b) a hand gesture recognition system; and b) a gestural flick input. The virtual keyboard is displayed on the monitor, and allows the user to input characters and to perform different gestural functions. The hand gesture recognition system detects and recognizes the area of the hand that is executing the different gesture functions, such as “newline”, “space”, “delete”, “backspace”, “open”, “close”, and “select language”. In the gestural flick input system, the user inputs the character without touching an input device. Figure 1 shows the general workflow of the proposed system.
3.1 Virtual keyboard
The system displays the virtual keyboard on the monitor. The virtual keyboard uses the flick input method, a Japanese input method used on smartphones that is now also used in other countries. Flick input is often used as a way to input characters on a smartphone, as it allows users to easily enter characters. In this system, users can choose a particular character instead of pressing a key repeatedly. Figure 2 represents a flick keyboard for the input of Japanese and English characters. In this work, the virtual keyboard contains two processes, a primary virtual keyboard (PVK) and a secondary virtual keyboard (SVK). The system displays the PVK on the monitor. The user inputs a character by using the PVK, as shown in the example in Fig. 3a. The PVK is divided into 13 blocks. The first 12 are characters of the flick character sequence, and are known as character blocks; the remaining one is known as a non-character block, and is used for the performance of different gesture functions. When the user performs gesture functions in character blocks via the PVK, the system displays the SVK to offer additional activity. The SVK presents a flick character sequence, such as a, i, u, e, and o, as shown in Fig. 3d. Five characters are used in each flick input sequence shown in Fig. 3e. Each character block in the PVK and SVK is expressed as a region of interest (ROI) location in order to capture the hand gesture images. This system supports a virtual keyboard offering three character sets: Japanese Hiragana, the English alphabet and numbers as

