3.3 Gestural Flick input
The gestural flick input is a non-touch character input method by which the user inputs characters through a virtual keyboard on the display screen. It is executed via recognition of the user’s hand gestures. In essence, the user can select and input a character from the character block of the virtual keyboard, using ‘open’ and ‘close’ gestures, and can perform different gesture functions via non-character blocks. The gestural flick input system is shown in Fig. 7, and involves the following procedures:
1. The user selects the first character of the flick character sequence by viewing the PVK and performing the ‘close’ gesture on that character block. The system displays SVK on the monitor.
2. The user selects the appropriate character by viewing the SVK and performing the ‘close’ gesture on that character block.
3. The selected character is input, and the system displays the PVK on the monitor. The user inputs characters by repeating these procedures.
The user also performs various hand gestures as gesture functions in the character input system. The user executes the gesture functions via the non-character block; these gesture functions are described in Table 2. The user is able to enter a new line by holding up the index finger, and can add a space by holding up two fingers (index and middle finger). When three fingers (index, middle and ring finger) are held up, the system deletes a previously input character. The backspace is executed when four fingers are displayed. The system detects an

