5 Discussion
In this study, we endeavor to recognize the character writing with seven typical hand gestures. The proposed system uses a virtual keyboard to produce characters, and the user input the character by performing the gesture at the ROI position on this keyboard. Based on the experimental results, the average accuracy of typical hand gestures is 98.09% as shown in Table 3. The recognition accuracy of each gesture was well-balanced to demonstrate the high reliability of the system. From the confusion matrix in Fig. 12, we identified some of the misrecognition of gesture recognition. The average misclassified gestures are 1.91% shown in Table 4. This type of error was caused by the gesture shape and size of the hand. Apparently distinguishable gestures like “backspace” and “open” can look the same when performing hand gestures. However, the ‘open’ and ‘close’ achieves highest accuracy where the ‘backspace’ is the lowest. Further, we evaluated various state-of-the-art algorithms using our dataset to compare the accuracy of hand gesture recognition shown in Table 5 and achieved 95.12%, 97.38% and 95.48%, respectively. We observed that the method [33] achieved the closest 97.38% to the achievement of our proposed method. This method used input images and segmented images for CNN’s double channels. However, the noise and segmentation process of the input images can have an impact on the accuracy. From the results, we can say that our

