6 Conclusion
In this paper, we present a non-touch character writing system based on hand gestures that provide a reliable and efficient interface for character input for the most demanding environments and applications, reducing the gap between humans, machines or robots, and allowing the user to operate the system in a healthy and safe way. The proposed method can play an effective role in gesture recognition and character inputs. Hand gesture recognition is the main concern of character input. From this point of view, this paper proposed a hand gesture recognition model where gesture images were captured from the region of interest (ROI) of the virtual keyboard. The gesture image was segmented to detect the hand area and the proposed CNN method was used to extract the gesture features. Finally, the gestures were recognized using SVM classification techniques. This hand gesture recognition system was incorporated in the proposed non-touch character input system. The user generates character input by viewing a virtual keyboard on a monitor using different hand gesture functions. A flick keyboard system was introduced to the virtual keyboard, through which the user can quickly and easily input characters. We used seven gestures and applied a robustness color segmentation scheme for detecting the hand. In order to verify our proposed method, an experimental setup was established in a laboratory environment, in which 25 volunteers participated. The hand gesture images are collected using a low-cost device like a webcam. The experimental results show that our system can achieve a high recognition accuracy in offline and real-time conditions of 98.09% and 96.23%, respectively, with seven general gesture functions, and average recognition accuracy for character input of 97.93%. It is robust in a real-time environment. Also, recognition accuracy shows satisfactory performances compared to the state-of-art method.
In the future, we will focus on reducing error rates and increasing performance over time compared to state-of-the-art systems, and add more typical hand gestures for character inputs with different languages that support greater functionality and instruction for advanced nontouch systems.

